{"abstract":"Project managers and planners are highly prone to believing that their projects are one of a kind—partly because those that seem new and distinctive are more likely to win support. But research on 1,300-plus projects reveals that few, if any, actually are unique. The problem is, the perception of uniqueness causes managers to think there’s nothing to learn from other projects, which leads them to underestimate risk, make poor decisions, and blow through budgets and schedules. The cure is to always assume that someone, somewhere has undertaken a project like yours. If you can’t find an analogue, break your project into components, which may prove comparable with other projects. Then use forecasting and other risk assessment tools to avoid biases that undermine good choices.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander","Zottoli, M.","Christodoulou, M.D."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798547"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Bias","Project Leadership","Project management"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the uniqueness trap","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://hbr.org/2025/03/the-uniqueness-trap","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798547","identifier":[{"id":"86000333044","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8fca6dde-db01-4a2e-b349-8d136f32379b"],"keyword":["Bias","Project Leadership","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-A085723BCAEB54DA403B9305449E1890"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://hbr.org/2025/03/the-uniqueness-trap","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Software Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"M.","key":"zottolipau","last":"Zottoli","name":"Zottoli, M.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"M.D.","key":"christodouloupau","last":"Christodoulou","name":"Christodoulou, M.D.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00178012"],"journal":"Harvard Business Review","status":"p","title":"Harvard Business Review","volume":"March-April"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000333044"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8fca6dde-db01-4a2e-b349-8d136f32379b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Uniqueness Trap","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798547","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This article measures and explains cost risk for IT projects compared with 22 other project types. The null hypothesis is that IT projects are not different from other projects in terms of cost risk. The thesis is falsified. IT cost risk is found to be uniquely more risky than other project types. First, we review four extant explanations of high IT cost risk: immaturity, intangibility, goal ambiguity, and stakeholder resistance. Second, we describe data to measure cost risk across 23 project types, including IT. Third, we fit theoretical distributions to the data and test for median and tail risk, showing that tail risk dominates, with IT cost risk having a fatter tail than any other project type. Fourth, we develop a taxonomy of risk based on the Pareto 1 tail parameter α, documenting that IT is uniquely risky as the only project type with α ≤ 1, indicating infinite mean and variance and, thus, infinite and unpredictable risk. Fifth, we return to the four extant explanations and conclude that, although our data do not prove the explanations, they also do not falsify them. Based on our data, we suggest two further explanations of cost risk: Bespokeness and “think-fast” decision-making, which we argue drive extreme risk and are typical of IT, with modularity and “think slow” as antidotes. Finally, we identify areas for further research.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander","Aaen, Jon","Keil, Mark","Zottoli, Mariagrazia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-823168"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Georgia State University","University of Oxford"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the uniqueness of it cost risk a cross group comparison of 000000000023 project types","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728251340590","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-823168","ids":["aau-0e1909f1-9b9a-408b-8e10-aca67712b30c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-60F5182B4CED129C9F12998E87D96B36"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728251340590","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, Centre for Information Systems Management","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, Public Administration & Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"georgia state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Georgia State University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jon","key":"aaenpau","last":"Aaen","locid":"3d81e322-04e5-4232-9352-9314d1576728","name":"Aaen, Jon","orcid":"0000-0003-2176-4678","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark","key":"keilpau","last":"Keil","name":"Keil, Mark","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mariagrazia","key":"zottolipau","last":"Zottoli","name":"Zottoli, Mariagrazia","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/87569728251340590","issn":["19389507","87569728"],"issn_org":["87569728"],"journal":"Project Management Journal","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"e","title":"Project Management Journal"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["aau-0e1909f1-9b9a-408b-8e10-aca67712b30c"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"The Uniqueness of IT Cost Risk: A Cross-Group Comparison of 23 Project Types","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-823168","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The paper explores \"uniqueness bias,\" a behavioral bias defined as the tendency of planners and managers to see their decisions as singular. For the first time, uniqueness bias is correlated with forecasting accuracy and performance in real-world project investment decisions. We problematize the conventional framing of projects as unique and hypothesize that it leads to poor project performance. We test the thesis for a sample of 219 projects and find that perceived uniqueness is indeed highly statistically significantly associated with underperformance. Finally, we identify how decision makers can mitigate uniqueness bias in their projects through what Daniel Kahneman aptly called \"decision hygiene,\" specifically reference class forecasting, premortems, similarity-based forecasting, and noise audits.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander","Christodoulou, M.D."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776409"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Behavioral economics","Cost Overruns","Decision bias","Decision making","Development","It","Program management","Project management","Risk","Uniqueness"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"uniqueness bias why it matters how to curb it","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4924942","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776409","ids":["itu-6a7cf049-5900-4849-a4fc-6ea50e371804"],"keyword":["Behavioral Economics","Cost Overruns","Decision Bias","Decision Making","Development","IT","Program Management","Project Management","Risk","Schedule Overruns","Uniqueness"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-645AEB4199782AB567437265BB207822"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4924942","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4924942","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106958301/Uniqueness_Bias_Why_it_matters_how_to_curb_it..pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"M.D.","key":"christodouloupau","last":"Christodoulou","name":"Christodoulou, M.D.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2139/ssrn.4924942","pages":"30","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-6a7cf049-5900-4849-a4fc-6ea50e371804"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Uniqueness Bias : Why It Matters, How to Curb It","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776409","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The present paper is an update and extension of the \"The Oxford Olympics Study 2016\" (Flyvbjerg et al. 2016). We document that the Games remain costly and continue to have large cost overruns, to a degree that threatens their viability. The IOC is aware of the problem and has initiated reform. We assess the reforms and find: (a) Olympic costs are statistically significantly increasing; prior analyses did not show this trend; it is a step in the wrong direction. (b) Cost overruns were decreasing until 2008, but have increased since then; again a step in the wrong direction. (c) At present, the cost of Paris 2024 is USD 8.7 billion (2022 level) and cost overrun is 115% in real terms; this is not the cheaper Games that were promised. (d) Cost overruns are the norm for the Games, past, present, and future; they are the only project type that never delivered on budget, ever. We assess a new IOC policy of reducing cost by reusing existing venues instead of building new ones. We find that reuse did not have the desired effect for Tokyo 2020 and also looks ineffective for Paris 2024. Finally, we recommend that the Games look to other types of megaprojects for better data, better forecasting, and how to generate the positive learning curves that are necessary for bringing costs and overrun down. Only if this happens are Los Angeles 2028 and Brisbane 2032 likely to live up to the IOC’s intentions of a more affordable Games that more cities will want to host.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776424"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Cost Overruns","Olympic Games","Olympics","Sports"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the oxford olympics study 000000002024 are cost and cost overrun at the games coming down","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4850085","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776424","ids":["itu-928a985d-914b-47ee-8ac8-68d6417d5cee"],"keyword":["Cost Overruns","Olympic Games","Olympics","Paris 2024","Sports"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-F13FB4C88A734B8CB9771B298198A885"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4850085","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4850085","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2139/ssrn.4850085","pages":"27","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-928a985d-914b-47ee-8ac8-68d6417d5cee"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Oxford Olympics Study 2024 : Are Cost and Cost Overrun at the Games Coming Down?","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776424","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Behavioral science has witnessed an explosion in the number of biases identified by behavioral scientists, to more than 200 at present. Bent Flyvbjerg identifies the most important behavioral biases for project management, including political and cognitive bias. Specifically, the chapter focuses on strategic misrepresentation, optimism bias, uniqueness bias, the planning fallacy, overconfidence bias, and the base-rate fallacy. Each bias is defined and its impacts on project management are explained, with examples. Base-rate neglect is identified as a primary reason that projects underperform. This is supported by the presentation of the most comprehensive set of base rates that exists in project management scholarship, from 2,062 projects. Finally, reference class forecasting, premortems, and decision hygiene are recommended as cures to bias.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776457"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"behavioral bias","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/05491b44-c4d9-3603-a6c8-c889adddfdf2/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776457","ids":["itu-efcd01ba-3542-48c4-9f62-a3f994fafee5"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-634EE42760397C45B9457A741BCA6E59"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Martina","key":"huemannpedh","last":"Huemann","name":"Huemann, Martina","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rodney","key":"turnerpedh","last":"Turner","name":"Turner, Rodney","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003274179-36","edition":"6","eisbn":["9781003274179"],"pages":"435-450","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"The Handbook of Project Management"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-efcd01ba-3542-48c4-9f62-a3f994fafee5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Behavioral bias","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776457","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Digitalization is a core component of the green transition. Today’s focus is on quantifying and predicting the climate effects of digitalization through various life-cycle assessments and baseline scenario methodologies. Here we argue that this is a mistake. Most attempts at prediction are based on three implicit assumptions: (a) the digital carbon footprint can be quantified, (b) business-as-usual with episodic change leading to a new era of stability, and (c) investments in digitalization will be delivered within the cost, timeframe, and benefits described in their business cases. We problematize each assumption within the context of digitalization and argue that the digital carbon footprint is inherently unpredictable. We build on uncertainty literature to show that even if you cannot predict, you can still mitigate. On that basis, we propose to rethink practice on the digital carbon footprint from prediction to mitigation.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Gritsenko, Daria","Aaen, Jon","Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747224"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Helsinki"],"keyword":["Climate change mitigation","Climate change policy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rethinking digitalization and climate don t predict mitigate","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44168-024-00127-z","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747224","ids":["itu-493b8bd6-df2f-4e6c-b415-4a8c76b2bb67"],"keyword":["Climate-change mitigation","Climate-change policy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E6BD75E0CDBD12BD7F49AC68A4541CA7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/105511429/Rethinking_digitalization_and_climate.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s44168-024-00127-z","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"university of helsinki","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Helsinki","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daria","key":"gritsenkopau","last":"Gritsenko","name":"Gritsenko, Daria","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jon","key":"aaenpau","last":"Aaen","locid":"6b81fcf4-173e-4e8b-86d6-2c9e7b4320c6","name":"Aaen, Jon","orcid":"0000-0003-2176-4678","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-493b8bd6-df2f-4e6c-b415-4a8c76b2bb67"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Rethinking digitalization and climate: don’t predict, mitigate","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747224","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Heuristics are fast-and-frugal rules of thumb, used to simplify complex decisions. This article explores how heuristics may help improve project leadership. First, it identifies two schools of thought in scholarship about heuristics. Second, it explains why heuristics work, based on Occam’s razor. Third, it outlines five steps for teasing out project leaders’ tacit heuristics, with examples of how the steps were employed in leadership training at Oxford University. The five steps emphasize the role of Aristotelian phronesis in developing effective heuristics. Fourth, the article discusses heuristics that drove success at Pixar and how we may learn from these. Finally, areas for further research are identified. Readers, whether practitioners or scholars, are encouraged to develop and improve their own leadership heuristics and guidance is given for how to do this.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776370"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Heuristics","Project Leadership","Project management"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"heuristics for better project leadership teasing out tacit knowledge","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728241300307","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776370","identifier":[{"id":"85211201158","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0ec0d1d8-3206-4869-b882-4bd94913223f"],"keyword":["Heuristics","Project Leadership","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-46999FB3B3BBCA929F2B3BFCE3E9C47B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/87569728241300307","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728241300307","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/87569728241300307","issn":["19389507","87569728"],"issn_org":["19389507"],"issue":"6","journal":"Project Management Journal","pages":"615-625","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Project Management Journal","volume":"55"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85211201158"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0ec0d1d8-3206-4869-b882-4bd94913223f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Heuristics for Better Project Leadership : Teasing Out Tacit Knowledge","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776370","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"AIMS: In patients with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), myocardial ketone metabolism is increased and short-term treatment with the ketone body 3-hydroxy butyrate (3-OHB) has beneficial haemodynamic effects. In patients with HFrEF, we investigated whether the level of circulating 3-OHB predicted all-cause mortality and sought to identify correlations between patient characteristics and circulating 3-OHB levels. METHODS AND RESULTS: We conducted a cohort study in 218 patients with HFrEF. Plasma 3-OHB levels were measured using high-performance liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry. Data on all-cause mortality were obtained by reviewing the patients' medical records, which are linked to the national 'Central Person Registry' that registers the timing of all deaths in the country. Mean left ventricular ejection fraction was 35 ± 8.6%, mean age was 67 ± 10 years, 54% were New York Heart Association II, and 27% had type 2 diabetes mellitus. Median follow-up time was 7.3 (interquartile range 6.3-8.4) years. We observed large variations in 3-OHB levels between patients (median 59 μM, range: 14-694 μM). Patients with 3-OHB levels above the median displayed a markedly increased risk of death compared with those with low levels {hazard ratio [HR]: 2.1 [95% confidence interval (CI): 1.3-3.5], P = 0.003}. In a multivariate analysis, 3-OHB predicted mortality independently of known chronic heart failure risk factors [HR: 1.004 (95% CI: 1.001-1.007), P = 0.02] and with a similar statistical strength as N-terminal pro-brain natriuretic peptide (NT-proBNP) [HR: 1.0005 (95% CI: 1.000-1.001), P = 0.02]. For every 100 μmol increase in plasma 3-OHB, the hazard of death increased by 49%. The following factors significantly predicted 3-OHB levels in the univariate analysis: free fatty acids (FFAs) [β: 238 (95% CI: 185-292), P < 0.0001], age [β: 2.43 (95% CI: 1.14-3.72), P < 0.0001], plasma insulin {β: -0.28 [95% CI: -0.54-(-0.02)], P = 0.036}, body mass index {β: -3.15 [95% CI: -5.26-(-0.05)], P = 0.046}, diabetes [β: 44.49 (95% CI: 14.84-74.14), P = 0.003], glycosylated haemoglobin [β: 1.92 (95% CI: 0.24-3.59), P = 0.025], New York Heart Association class [β: 26.86 (95% CI: 5.99-47.72), P = 0.012], and NT-proBNP [β: 0.03 (95% CI: 0.01-0.04), P = 0.001]. In a multivariate analysis, only FFAs predicted 3-OHB levels [β: 216 (95% CI: 165-268), P > 0.001]. CONCLUSIONS: In patients with HFrEF, circulating 3-OHB was a strong predictor of all-cause mortality independently of NT-proBNP. Circulating FFAs were the best predictor of 3-OHB levels.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Christensen, Kristian Hylleberg","Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Schou, Morten","Gustafsson, Ida","Jorsal, Anders","Flyvbjerg, Allan","Tarnow, Lise","Bøtker, Hans Erik","Kistorp, Caroline","Johannsen, Mogens","Møller, Niels","Wiggers, Henrik"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-724779"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Christensen, Kristian Hylleberg"],"keyword":["3 Hydroxy butyrate","3 Hydroxybutyric Acid","Aged","Cohort Studies","Diabetes Mellitus Type 2","Heart failure","Humans","Ketone bodies","Metabolism","Middle Aged","Prognosis","Stroke Volume","Ventricular Function Left"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"circulating 000000000003 hydroxy butyrate predicts mortality in patients with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ehf2.14476","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-724779","identifier":[{"id":"38196294","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10966261","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85181658453","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-a14cb22a-3a56-49d2-818f-75c5c89c0d48","ku-731ac69d-5536-4bd7-b842-cc3ecad024bb"],"keyword":["3-Hydroxy butyrate","3-Hydroxybutyric Acid","Aged","Cohort Studies","Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2","Heart failure","Humans","Ketone bodies","Metabolism","Middle Aged","Prognosis","Stroke Volume","Ventricular Function, Left"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-7F083B2D2D7890B8EA047452C5527375"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/388637344/ESC_Heart_Failure_2024_Christensen_Circulating_3_hydroxy_butyrate_predicts_mortality_in_patients_with_chronic_heart.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ehf2.14476","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal medicine: Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Herlev and Gentofte"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"dk","key":"steno diabetes centers","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-STENO","l3nn":"Steno Diabetes Centers","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Steno Diabetes Centers","namevar":["Steno Diabetes Centre Zealand"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-STENO","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristian Hylleberg","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"253e284b-a123-4af7-894e-8d3d13662de5","name":"Christensen, Kristian Hylleberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Roni R","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"896f4a21-d29c-4ad9-b0ef-4e7f8616c234","name":"Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","name_org":"Nielsen, Roni R","orcid":"0000-0001-9228-3869","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","first":"Morten","key":"schoupau","last":"Schou","locid":"48fa6ec9-1941-4566-b931-14b0cc8cbae3","name":"Schou, Morten","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Ida","key":"gustafssonpau","last":"Gustafsson","name":"Gustafsson, Ida","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"jorsalpau","last":"Jorsal","locid":"16e0eff8-d885-41bc-afb7-89c374f7c0c9","name":"Jorsal, Anders","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Allan","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","name":"Flyvbjerg, Allan","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Lise","key":"tarnowpau","last":"Tarnow","name":"Tarnow, Lise","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hans Erik","key":"bøtkerpau","last":"Bøtker","locid":"09b03c00-11a8-4f72-800f-181f26155750","name":"Bøtker, Hans Erik","orcid":"0000-0001-6358-8962","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","first":"Caroline","key":"kistorppau","last":"Kistorp","locid":"42328645-d772-4f53-9f70-438ff40291b9","name":"Kistorp, Caroline","orcid":"0000-0002-3019-6775","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mogens","key":"johannsenpau","last":"Johannsen","locid":"75805917-240a-47fb-8c5f-ef88bb000b43","name":"Johannsen, Mogens","orcid":"0000-0002-2548-7025","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"2e0d8e2a-2da1-4d55-b2da-0a25c0375b1e","name":"Møller, Niels","orcid":"0000-0001-5627-7322","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"wiggerspau","last":"Wiggers","locid":"aef6a13e-8c07-474c-a4f6-bc35236a6eda","name":"Wiggers, Henrik","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38196294"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ehf2.14476","issn":["20555822"],"issue":"2","journal":"ESC Heart Failure","pages":"837-845","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"ESC Heart Failure","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85181658453"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a14cb22a-3a56-49d2-818f-75c5c89c0d48","ku-731ac69d-5536-4bd7-b842-cc3ecad024bb"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Circulating 3-hydroxy butyrate predicts mortality in patients with chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-724779","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The secrets to successfully planning and delivering ambitious, complex projects on any scale—from home renovation to space exploration—by the world’s leading expert on megaprojects.“Important, timely, instructive, and entertaining.”—Daniel Kahneman, Nobel Prize–winning author of Thinking, Fast and Slow Nothing is more inspiring than a big vision that becomes a triumphant, new reality. Think of how the Empire State Building went from a sketch to the jewel of New York’s skyline in twenty-one months, or how Apple’s iPod went from a project with a single employee to a product launch in eleven months. These are wonderful stories. But most of the time big visions turn into nightmares. Remember Boston’s “Big Dig”? Almost every sizeable city in the world has such a fiasco in its backyard. In fact, no less than 92% of megaprojects come in over budget or over schedule, or both. The cost of California’s high-speed rail project soared from $33 billion to $100 billon—and won’t even go where promised. More modest endeavors, whether launching a small business, organizing a conference, or just finishing a work project on time, also commonly fail. Why? Understanding what distinguishes the triumphs from the failures has been the life’s work of Oxford professor Bent Flyvbjerg, dubbed “the world’s leading megaproject expert.” In How Big Things Get Done, he identifies the errors in judgment and decision-making that lead projects, both big and small, to fail, and the research-based principles that will make you succeed with yours. For example:• Understand your odds. If you don’t know them, you won’t win.• Plan slow, act fast. Getting to the action …","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Gardner, Dan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384464"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Ottawa"],"keyword":["Budget overruns","Decision making errors","Megaproject management","Project planning","Project success principles"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how big things get done the surprising factors that determine the fate of every project from home renovations to space exploration and everything in between","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384464","ids":["itu-d9120226-e4f9-459e-84f8-db433fbde4ba"],"keyword":["Budget overruns","Decision-making errors","Megaproject management","Project planning","Project success principles"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FCE406787C6A016033C20196DB3605A9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of ottawa","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Ottawa","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dan","key":"gardnerpau","last":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Dan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9780593239513"],"publisher":"Penguin Random House","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d9120226-e4f9-459e-84f8-db433fbde4ba"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Big Things Get Done : The Surprising Factors that Determine the Fate of Every Project from Home Renovations to Space Exploration, and Everything in Between","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384464","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The secrets to successfully planning and delivering ambitious, complex projects on any scale—from home renovation to space exploration—by the world's leading expert on megaprojects. Nothing is more inspiring than a big vision that becomes a triumphant, new reality. Think of how the Empire State Building went from a sketch to the jewel of New York's skyline in twenty-one months, or how Apple’s iPod went from a project with a single employee to a product launch in eleven months. These are wonderful stories. But most of the time big visions turn into nightmares. Remember Boston’s “Big Dig”? Almost every sizeable city in the world has such a fiasco in its backyard. In fact, no less than 92% of megaprojects come in over budget or over schedule, or both. The cost of California’s high-speed rail project soared from $33 billion to $100 billon—and won’t even go where promised. More modest endeavors, whether launching a small business, organizing a conference, or just finishing a work project on time, also commonly fail. Why? Understanding what distinguishes the triumphs from the failures has been the life’s work of Oxford professor Bent Flyvbjerg, dubbed “the world’s leading megaproject expert.” In How Big Things Get Done, he identifies the errors in judgment and decision-making that lead projects, both big and small, to fail, and the research-based principles that will make you succeed with yours. For example: Understand your odds. If you don't know them, you won't win. Plan slow, act fast. Getting to the action quick feels right. But it's wrong. Think right to left. Start with your goal, then identify the steps to get there. Find your Lego. Big is best …","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Gardner, Dan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712491"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Ottawa"],"keyword":["Budget overruns","Decision making","Megaprojects","Project Management Principles","Project planning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"como fazer grandes coisas the surprising factors that determine the fate of every project from home renovations to space exploration and everything in between","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-712491","ids":["itu-05a67f27-280d-42e2-af9b-9eb5bec6a3ed"],"keyword":["Budget Overruns","Decision-Making","Megaprojects","Project Management Principles","Project Planning"],"lang":"Portuguese","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5E5EDAC9DF73AD1E80D4A4D44705C305"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of ottawa","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Ottawa","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dan","key":"gardnerpau","last":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Dan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9786550473853"],"pages":"373","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-05a67f27-280d-42e2-af9b-9eb5bec6a3ed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Como fazer grandes coisas : The Surprising Factors that Determine the Fate of Every Project from Home Renovations to Space Exploration, and Everything in Between","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712491","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"A study of some 16,000 major projects—from large buildings to bridges, dams, power stations, rockets, railroads, information technology systems, and even the Olympic Games—reveals a massive project-management problem. Only 8.5% of those projects were delivered on time and on budget, while a mere 0.5% were completed on time and on budget and produced the expected benefits. In other words, 99.5% of large projects failed to deliver as promised. Master architect Frank Gehry consistently defies those odds, producing projects of staggering beauty while meeting time and budget targets. This article reveals four lessons, gleaned from Gehry and his colleagues, for successfully managing big projects.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Gardner, Dan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730186"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Ottawa"],"keyword":["Architecture","Digital twin","Project Leadership","Project management"],"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how frank gehry delivers on time and on budget lessons from the master architect in managing big projects","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dna","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://hbr.org/2023/01/how-frank-gehry-delivers-on-time-and-on-budget","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-730186","ids":["itu-a378a25a-6c04-45c4-a3bc-2ab04cd4eef7"],"keyword":["Architecture","Digital Twin","Project Leadership","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B24A10B83E6F172C95D43BF2F06C12EB"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of ottawa","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Ottawa","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dan","key":"gardnerpau","last":"Gardner","name":"Gardner, Dan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00178012"],"issue":"January-February","newspaper":"Harvard Business Review","status":"p","title":"Harvard Business Review","volume":"2023"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a378a25a-6c04-45c4-a3bc-2ab04cd4eef7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Frank Gehry Delivers on Time and on Budget: Lessons from the Master Architect in Managing Big Projects","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730186","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Private and public megaprojects—whether new plant facilities, IT systems, railways, or the Olympics—frequently entail dramatic cost and schedule overruns. Root causes are behavioral biases, such as optimism and deliberate deception, accompanied by principal–agent issues and a lack of project-related skills. Through a three-stage process—forecasting, organizing, and executing (“FOX”)—we organize and offer solutions to mitigate the cognitive biases and agency issues planners and policy-makers face in large projects. Following the FOX process and building on behavioral decision theory, we review evidence for the accuracy of “reference-class forecasting,” which considers comparable past projects to forecast a current, planned project. We provide evidence for reference-class forecasting performance and recent methodological extensions, such as similarity-based forecasting. Then, considering the relevant literature, we offer organizational solutions to reduce unfounded optimism and deception, including debiasing techniques and specific measures to curb principal–agent issues. Finally, we suggest combining a project modular design with speedy implementation for faster, better, cheaper, and lower-risk execution. Overall, we offer an original, holistic theoretical view that addresses both behavioral and strategic elements of how to debias large projects, along with direct practical implications and advice for those who manage megaprojects with increasingly high stakes and risks.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Lovallo, Dan","Cristofaro, Matteo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712516"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Sydney","University of Rome Tor Vergata"],"keyword":["Behavioral biases","Megaprojects","Principal agent issues","Project modular design","Reference class forecasting"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"governing large projects a three stage process to get it right","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-712516","identifier":[{"id":"85149733875","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-47e8bea2-5532-402f-a605-10e77065bf45"],"keyword":["Behavioral biases","Megaprojects","Principal–agent issues","Project modular design","Reference-class forecasting"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-54877680CF0AE6528C68A3485B9E1627"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"the university of sydney","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"The University of Sydney","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of rome tor vergata","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Rome Tor Vergata","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dan","key":"lovallopau","last":"Lovallo","name":"Lovallo, Dan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Matteo","key":"cristofaropau","last":"Cristofaro","name":"Cristofaro, Matteo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-47e8bea2-5532-402f-a605-10e77065bf45"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Governing Large Projects: A Three-Stage Process to Get It Right","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712516","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"How should government and business leaders solve big problems? Ought policy responses to occur in bold leaps or multitudinous methodical moves? Here we show that one-off major projects, with a high level of bespoke content, are prone systematically to poorer outcomes than projects built with a repeatable platform strategy. Repeatable projects are cheaper, faster, and scale in volume and variety at much lower risk of failure. We arrive at these conclusions using comparative evidence—NASA vs SpaceX—on cost, speed-to-market and schedule, and scalability outcomes of their respective space missions. Our reference class dataset consists of 203 space missions spanning 1963–2021, of which 181 missions belong to NASA and 22 belong to SpaceX. We find that SpaceX’s platform strategy was 10X cheaper and 2X faster than NASA’s bespoke strategy. Moreover, SpaceX’s platform strategy was less risky, virtually eliminating cost overruns. We further show that achieving platform repeatability is a strategically diligent process involving experimental learning sequences. Sectors of the economy where governments find it difficult to control spending or timeframes or to get benefits quickly enough—e.g. health, education, climate, defence—are ripe for a platform rethink.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Ansar, Atif","Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381556"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Cost Overruns","Major Projects","Megaprojects","Payload","Platforms","Public policy","Rocket Launches","Scalability","Scale up","Space missions","Speed To Market","Strateg"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how to solve big problems bespoke versus platform strategies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/oxrep/grac009","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381556","identifier":[{"id":"85132212821","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-134adcf8-6d20-4b44-b5ec-b405b31b8fe3"],"keyword":["Cost Overruns","Major Projects","Megaprojects","Payload","Platforms","Public Policy","Rocket Launches","Scalability","Scale-up","Space Missions","Speed-To-Market","Strateg"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4548F0579FCD80E14B233C4896339EB5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333635/SpaceXAAM.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://academic.oup.com/oxrep/article-pdf/38/2/338/48298496/grac009.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/oxrep/grac009","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Bent-Flyvbjerg/publication/361118474_How_to_Solve_Big_Problems_Bespoke_Versus_Platform_Strategies_Bent_Flyvbjerg/links/629e4627c660ab61f8671ead/How-to-Solve-Big-Problems-Bespoke-Versus-Platform-Strategies-Bent-Flyvbjerg.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Atif","key":"ansarpau","last":"Ansar","name":"Ansar, Atif","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/oxrep/grac009","issn":["0266903X","14602121"],"issn_org":["14602121"],"issue":"2","journal":"Oxford Review of Economic Policy","pages":"338-368","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Oxford Review of Economic Policy","volume":"38"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132212821"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-134adcf8-6d20-4b44-b5ec-b405b31b8fe3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How to Solve Big Problems: Bespoke versus Platform Strategies","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381556","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"If managers assume a normal or near-normal distribution of Information Technology (IT) project cost overruns, as is common, and cost overruns can be shown to follow a power-law distribution, managers may be unwittingly exposing their organizations to extreme risk by severely underestimating the probability of large cost overruns. In this research, we collect and analyze a large sample comprised of 5,392 IT projects to empirically examine the probability distribution of IT project cost overruns. Further, we propose and examine a mechanism that can explain such a distribution. Our results reveal that IT projects are far riskier in terms of cost than normally assumed by decision makers and scholars. Specifically, we found that IT project cost overruns follow a power-law distribution in which there are a large number of projects with relatively small overruns and a fat tail that includes a smaller number of projects with extreme overruns. A possible generative mechanism for the identified power-law distribution is found in interdependencies among technological compo- nents in IT systems. We propose and demonstrate, through computer simulation, that a problem in a single technological component can lead to chain reactions in which other interdependent components are affected, causing substantial overruns. What the power law tells us is that extreme IT project cost overruns will occur and that the prevalence of these will be grossly underestimated if managers assume that overruns follow a normal or near-normal distribution. This underscores the impor- tance of realistically assessing and mitigating the cost risk of new IT projects up front.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander","Lee, Jong Seok","Keil, Mark","Lunn, Daniel","Bester, Dirk W."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381957"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Georgia State University","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","University of Oxford","University of Tennessee"],"keyword":["Fat tails","IT project cost overrun","IT project management","component interdependence","elf organized criticality","power law distribution","technological component"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the empirical reality of it project cost overrun discovering a power law distribution","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2022.2096544","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381957","identifier":[{"id":"85136905096","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2edf85c0-51c1-4352-b188-5cd2bcabe7af"],"keyword":["IT project cost overrun","IT project management","component interdependence","elf-organized criticality","fat-tails","power-law distribution","technological component"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-3EADE2CD7ED1A912D82E2D8A9EB7B95F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101336774/The_Empirical_Reality_of_IT_Project_Cost_Overruns_Discovering_A_Power_Law_Distribution.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/epdf/10.1080/07421222.2022.2096544?needAccess=true&role=button","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2022.2096544","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of tennessee","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Tennessee","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"georgia state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Georgia State University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jong Seok","key":"leepau","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jong Seok","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark","key":"keilpau","last":"Keil","name":"Keil, Mark","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"lunnpau","last":"Lunn","name":"Lunn, Daniel","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Dirk W.","key":"besterpau","last":"Bester","name":"Bester, Dirk W.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/07421222.2022.2096544","issn":["07421222","1557928X"],"issn_org":["07421222"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Management Information Systems","pages":"607-639","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","status":"p","title":"Journal of Management Information Systems","volume":"39"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2edf85c0-51c1-4352-b188-5cd2bcabe7af"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Empirical Reality of IT Project Cost Overrun : Discovering A Power-Law Distribution","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381957","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Most cost-benefit analyses assume that the estimates of costs and benefits are more or less accurate and unbiased. But what if, in reality, estimates of costs and benefits are highly inaccurate and biased? Then the assumption that cost-benefit analysis is a rational way to improve resource allocation would be a fallacy. Using the largest dataset of its kind, we test the assumption that cost and benefit estimates of infrastructure capital investments are accurate and unbiased. We find this is not the case with overwhelming statistical significance. We document the extent of cost overruns, demand shortfalls, and forecasting bias in infrastructure investments. We further assess whether such forecasting inaccuracies seriously distort effective resource allocation, which is found to be the case. We explain our findings in behavioral terms and explore their implications for infrastructure economics and policy. Finally, we conclude that cost-benefit analysis stands in need of reform, and we outline four steps to such reform.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Bester, Dirk W."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383285"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Behavioral Science","Behavioral economics","Cost Benefit Analysis","Cost benefit Fallacy","Forecasting","Infrastructure Economics","Infrastructure Investment","Infrastructure Policy","Resource allocation","Welfare Economics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how in accurate is cost benefit analysis data explanations and suggestions for reform","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Bent-Flyvbjerg/publication/358145554_How_InAccurate_Is_Cost-Benefit_Analysis_Data_Explanations_and_Suggestions_for_Reform/links/61f2b6bbc5e3103375c4c62d/How-InAccurate-Is-Cost-Benefit-Analysis-Data-Explanations-and-Suggestions-for-Reform.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383285","ids":["itu-89d2df84-a6dc-4fb8-83de-44a48d575267"],"keyword":["Behavioral Economics","Behavioral Science","Cost-benefit Analysis","Cost-benefit Fallacy","Forecasting","Infrastructure Economics","Infrastructure Investment","Infrastructure Policy","Resource Allocation","Welfare Economics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-9BCCF1EEF037F3EBADCE6701C95302E5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Bent-Flyvbjerg/publication/358145554_How_InAccurate_Is_Cost-Benefit_Analysis_Data_Explanations_and_Suggestions_for_Reform/links/61f2b6bbc5e3103375c4c62d/How-InAccurate-Is-Cost-Benefit-Analysis-Data-Explanations-and-Suggestions-for-Reform.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dirk W.","key":"besterpau","last":"Bester","name":"Bester, Dirk W.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781558444188"],"pages":"174-196","place":"Cambridge, MA","publisher":"Lincoln Institute of Land Policy","status":"p","title":"Infrastructure Economics and Policy: International Perspectives"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-89d2df84-a6dc-4fb8-83de-44a48d575267"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How (In)Accurate Is Cost-Benefit Analysis? Data, Explanations, and Suggestions for Reform","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383285","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Heuristics are fast and frugal rules of thumb, used to simplify complex decisions. First, the paper iden¬tifies two schools of thought in heuristics scholarship, one of positive and one of negative heuristics. Second, the paper explains why heuristics work, based on Occam's razor. Third, it outlines five steps for teasing out project leaders' tacit heuristics, illustrating each step with real-life examples. The five steps emphasize the role of Aristotelian phronesis in developing effective heuristics. Fourth, the paper gives examples of project leaders who have deliberated about their heuristics and made them explicit. Finally, the author presents his own heuristics to illustrate in detail how heuristics can work in practice to improve project leadership. Readers are encouraged to develop and improve their own heuristics, and guidance is given for how to do this.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382979"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Behavioral economics","Fat tails","Heuristics","Masterbuilders","Modularity","Outside View","Phronesis","Positive Learning","Project management"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"heuristics for masterbuilders fast and frugal ways to become a better project leader","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4159984","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382979","ids":["itu-73a1bdd1-23c0-4172-89b8-8cebb60e3334"],"keyword":["Behavioral Economics","Fat Tails","Heuristics","Masterbuilders","Modularity","Outside View","Phronesis","Positive Learning","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-65F09D6993AD2FD403D00C29E58DB4CD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4159984","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"29","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-73a1bdd1-23c0-4172-89b8-8cebb60e3334"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Heuristics for Masterbuilders: Fast and Frugal Ways to Become a Better Project Leader","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382979","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Hong Kong has a large volume of construction works currently taking place; this will increase further and faster in the coming years. Hong Kong’s projects typically come in 15% under budget. However, ten per cent of local projects over-run their budgets, with a total overspend of eight percent. Some flagship projects have over-run their budgets and completion dates, causing public embarrassment. It is considered crucial to get these cost and schedule estimates under control. We investigated whether Artificial Intelligence (AI) can play a part in accurately predicting these outcomes and in identifying ahead of time when a project is going off track, allowing early intervention.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Budzier, Alexander","Chun-kit, Ricky LAU","Agard, Karlene","Leed, Andreas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381476"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford"],"keyword":["AI","Artificial intelligence","Cost Control","Cost overrun","Prediction","Project Leadership","Project management"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ai in action how the hong kong development bureau built the pss an early warning sign system for public works projects","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4192906","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381476","ids":["itu-0e210176-5ade-4d3c-92bc-7a4e36434d2c"],"keyword":["AI","Artificial Intelligence","Cost Control","Cost overrun","Prediction","Project Leadership","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C305AB626DB7CFD3B097791D747EC49D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4192906","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"budzierpau","last":"Budzier","name":"Budzier, Alexander","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ricky LAU","key":"chunkitpau","last":"Chun-kit","name":"Chun-kit, Ricky LAU","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Karlene","key":"agardpau","last":"Agard","name":"Agard, Karlene","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"leedpau","last":"Leed","name":"Leed, Andreas","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0e210176-5ade-4d3c-92bc-7a4e36434d2c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"AI in Action: How the Hong Kong Development Bureau Built the PSS, an Early-Warning-Sign System for Public Works Projects","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381476","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Private rocket companies like SpaceX have driven launch costs so far down that they have increased demand for services in space. The secret to their success is that they treat rocket systems as platforms rather than as bespoke, one-off projects. Having standard and reusable components makes it easier to extend the functionality of rocket systems and the reusability of the components enables these companies to benefit from constant incremental improvement. It’s an approach that may well help businesses face other major challenges.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Ansar, Atif"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384619"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saïd Business School, University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Incremental improvement","Launch costs","Private rocket companies","Reusable components","SpaceX"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a platform approach to space exploration","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4286513","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384619","ids":["itu-e3ae6332-30de-4f9d-9f62-d444a00ee846"],"keyword":["Incremental improvement","Launch costs","Private rocket companies","Reusable components","SpaceX"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-1297853A375EFC0A8B03A8F9822E0F75"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/SSRN_ID4286513_code1935014.pdf?abstractid=4286513&mirid=1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4286513","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102597625/SSRN-id4286513.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"saïd business school university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Saïd Business School, University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Atif","key":"ansarpau","last":"Ansar","name":"Ansar, Atif","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2139/ssrn.4286513","issn":["00178012"],"journal":"Harvard Business Review","status":"p","title":"Harvard Business Review"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e3ae6332-30de-4f9d-9f62-d444a00ee846"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Platform Approach to Space Exploration","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384619","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Behavioral science has witnessed an explosion in the number of biases identified by behavioral scientists, to more than 200 at present. This article identifies the 10 most important behavioral biases for project management. First, we argue it is a mistake to equate behavioral bias with cognitive bias, as is common. Cognitive bias is half the story; political bias the other half. Second, we list the top 10 behavioral biases in project management: (1) strategic misrepresentation, (2) optimism bias, (3) uniqueness bias, (4) the planning fallacy, (5) overconfidence bias, (6) hindsight bias, (7) availability bias, (8) the base rate fallacy, (9) anchoring, and (10) escalation of commitment. Each bias is defined, and its impacts on project management are explained, with examples. Third, base rate neglect is identified as a primary reason that projects underperform. This is supported by presentation of the most comprehensive set of base rates that exist in project management scholarship, from 2,062 projects. Finally, recent findings of power law outcomes in project performance are identified as a possible first stage in discovering a general theory of project management, with more fundamental and more scientific explanations of project outcomes than found in conventional theory.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382639"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Anchoring","Base rate Fallacy","Behavioral Science","Behavioral economics","Cognitive bias","Hindsight Bias","Optimism bias","Overconfidence Bias","Planning fallacy","Political Bias","Project management","Strategic Misrepresentation","Uniqueness Bias"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"top ten behavioral biases in project management an overview","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728211049046","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382639","identifier":[{"id":"85121400404","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5c1754a5-09e3-4f20-bcd8-ccebe54fadbf"],"keyword":["Anchoring","Availability Bias","Base-rate Fallacy","Behavioral Bias","Behavioral Economics","Behavioral Science","Cognitive Bias","Escalati","Escalation of Commitment","Hindsight Bias","Optimism Bias","Overconfidence Bias","Planning Fallacy","Political Bias","Project Management","Strategic Misrepresentation","Uniqueness Bias"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7397AEABC3055E321BCDA6347DF4E54C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/87569728211049046","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/epub/10.1177/87569728211049046","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/87569728211049046","issn":["19389507","87569728"],"issn_org":["19389507"],"issue":"6","journal":"Project Management Journal","pages":"531-546","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Project Management Journal","volume":"52"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121400404"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5c1754a5-09e3-4f20-bcd8-ccebe54fadbf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Top Ten Behavioral Biases in Project Management: An Overview","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382639","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Most cost-benefit analyses assume that the estimates of costs and benefits are more or less accurate and unbiased. But what if, in reality, estimates are highly inaccurate and biased? Then the assumption that cost-benefit analysis is a rational way to improve resource allocation would be a fallacy. Based on the largest dataset of its kind, we test the assumption that cost and benefit estimates of public investments are accurate and unbiased. We find this is not the case with overwhelming statistical significance. We document the extent of cost overruns, benefit shortfalls, and forecasting bias in public investments. We further assess whether such inaccuracies seriously distort effective resource allocation, which is found to be the case. We explain our findings in behavioral terms and explore their policy implications. Finally, we conclude that cost-benefit analysis of public investments stands in need of reform and we outline four steps to such reform.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent","Bester, Dirk W."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383908"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Behavioral Science","Behavioral economics","Cost Benefit Analysis","Cost benefit Fallacy","D60","D61","Forecasting","H00","H54","H80","Public Investment Planning","Resource allocation","Welfare Economics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the cost benefit fallacy why cost benefit analysis is broken and how to fix it","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/bca.2021.9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383908","identifier":[{"id":"85117361153","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b52fb0a9-562e-43e5-abaf-71868704ba63"],"keyword":["Behavioral Economics","Behavioral Science","Cost-benefit Analysis","Cost-benefit Fallacy","D60","D61","Forecasting","H00","H54","H80","Public Investment Planning","Resource Allocation","Welfare Economics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3789BA7622C9D639D0E9B8E38D7CB6B6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/bca.2021.9","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-benefit-cost-analysis/article/costbenefit-fallacy-why-costbenefit-analysis-is-broken-and-how-to-fix-it/608C8A0D37D38653846B9CF9DBC1DB49","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dirk W.","key":"besterpau","last":"Bester","name":"Bester, Dirk W.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/bca.2021.9","issn":["21522812"],"issn_org":["21945888"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Benefit-Cost Analysis","pages":"395-419","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Benefit-Cost Analysis","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85117361153"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b52fb0a9-562e-43e5-abaf-71868704ba63"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Cost-Benefit Fallacy: Why Cost-Benefit Analysis Is Broken and How to Fix It","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383908","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Oral delivery is a highly preferred method for drug administration due to high patient compliance. However, oral administration is intrinsically challenging for pharmacologically interesting drug classes, in particular pharmaceutical peptides, due to the biological barriers associated with the gastrointestinal tract. In this review, we start by summarizing the pharmacological performance of several clinically relevant orally administrated therapeutic peptides, highlighting their low bioavailabilities. Thus, there is a strong need to increase the transport of peptide drugs across the intestinal barrier to realize future treatment needs and further development in the field. Currently, progress is hampered by a lack of understanding of transport mechanisms that govern intestinal absorption and transport of peptide drugs, including the effects of the permeability enhancers commonly used to mediate uptake. We describe how, for the past decades, mechanistic insights have predominantly been gained using functional assays with end-point read-out capabilities, which only allow indirect study of peptide transport mechanisms. We then focus on fluorescence imaging that, on the other hand, provides opportunities to directly visualize and thus follow peptide transport at high spatiotemporal resolution. Consequently, it may provide new and detailed mechanistic understanding of the interplay between the physicochemical properties of peptides and cellular processes; an interplay that determines the efficiency of transport. We review current methodology and state of the art in the field of fluorescence imaging to study intestinal barrier transport of peptides, and provide a comprehensive overview of the imaging-compatible in vitro, ex vivo, and in vivo platforms that currently are being developed to accelerate this emerging field of research. This journal is","author_count":16,"contributors":["Larsen, Jannik B.","Taebnia, Nayere","Dolatshahi-Pirouz, Alireza","Eriksen, Anne Zebitz","Hjørringgaard, Claudia","Kristensen, Kasper","Larsen, Nanna Wichmann","Larsen, Niels Bent","Marie, Rodolphe","Mündler, Ann Kathrin","Parhamifar, Ladan","Urquhart, Andrew J.","Weller, Arjen","Mortensen, Kim I.","Flyvbjerg, Henrik","Andresen, Thomas Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-347672"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Andresen, Thomas Lars"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"imaging therapeutic peptide transport across intestinal barriers","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1039/d1cb00024a","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was supported by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (Grant No. NNF16OC0022166)."],"id":"S01-000.00-347672","identifier":[{"id":"85112166684","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8341777","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34458827","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-9085cd7f-5b8d-480e-979e-160ce8209504"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B3CE594199C25C9D316CEC335A50FD41"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1039/d1cb00024a","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/264652801/d1cb00024a.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["