esen, Thomas Lars","orcid":"0000-0002-1048-127X","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34458827"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1039/d1cb00024a","issn":["26330679"],"issue":"4","journal":"RSC Chemical Biology","pages":"1115-1143","publisher":"Royal Society of Chemistry","status":"p","title":"RSC Chemical Biology","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112166684"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-9085cd7f-5b8d-480e-979e-160ce8209504"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Imaging therapeutic peptide transport across intestinal barriers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-347672","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In conventional business and government megaprojects—such as hydroelectric dams, chemical-processing plants, or big-bang enterprise-resource-planning systems—the standard approach is to build something monolithic and customized. Such projects must be 100% complete before they can deliver benefits: Even when it’s 95% complete, a nuclear reactor is of no use. In this article, the author argues that two factors play a critical role in determining success or failure: modularity in design and speed in iteration. The article examines those factors by looking at well-known megaprojects, both successful ones and cautionary tales. It argues that \"What's our LEGO?\" is a key question for big projects. If you don't have a LEGO, you need to find one, or your project will be slow, expensive, and failure prone. If you have one, replicate and learn, over and over, to shorten delivery, bring down costs, and get to your revenues and benefits sooner.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Flyvbjerg, Bent"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383144"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Modularity","Negative Learning","Positive Learning","Replicability","Scale up","Speed"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"make megaprojects more modular","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3937465","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383144","identifier":[{"id":"85135091514","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-80302cd6-6a08-4133-be96-ab717ff33a15"],"keyword":["Modularity","Negative Learning","Positive Learning","Replicability","Scale-up","Speed"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-804A0ED2A06EFA9A37DC94C3888B15F3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://hbr.org/2021/11/make-megaprojects-more-modular","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://deliverypdf.ssrn.com/delivery.php?ID=596097013121101031070122002113122066063063059088049089096031112108101083088002085064036119005126028023035010072007004009064019114034029069015072031107075090127078088062043066067123097028074019001081122068125095090100070095024112064067105096098021013094&EXT=pdf&INDEX=TRUE","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=3937465","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"flyvbjergpau","last":"Flyvbjerg","locid":"598de448-af84-43c8-9147-6134a63c9b3c","name":"Flyvbjerg, Bent","orcid":"0000-0001-9093-770X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00178012"],"issue":"November-December","journal":"Harvard Business Review","pages":"58-63","status":"p","title":"Harvard Business Review"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-80302cd6-6a08-4133-be96-ab717ff33a15"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Make Megaprojects More Modular","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383144","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aim: To assess the effect of liraglutide, a glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist, on urinary sodium excretion as well as on circulating adrenomedullin and copeptin levels in patients with type 2 diabetes (T2D). Materials and methods: In the LIVE study, patients (n = 241) with left ventricular ejection fraction ≤45% were randomized to liraglutide 1.8 mg daily or placebo for 24 weeks, and 30% had a concomitant diagnosis of T2D. Plasma levels of N-terminal brain-natriuretic-peptide (NT-proBNP) (a predefined secondary endpoint), midregional pro-atrial-natriuretic-peptide (MR-proANP), midregional pro-adrenomedullin (MR-proADM) and copeptin were measured at baseline and after 24 weeks in this substudy. The potential effect modification of T2D was assessed. Results: In the eligible subgroup of 231 patients with available biomarkers (115 randomized to liraglutide and 116 to placebo), MR-proANP decreased by 12% (P =.002) and NT-proBNP by 9% (P =.009) during liraglutide treatment compared with placebo at week 24. Interaction with T2D for the treatment effect of change in MR-proANP and NT-proBNP levels was P =.003 and P =.03, respectively. Consequently, in patients with T2D, liraglutide decreased MR-proANP by 27% (P <.001) and NT-proBNP by 25% (P =.02) compared with placebo, whereas no change was observed in patients without T2D. There was no effect of liraglutide on MR-proADM (P =.10) or copeptin (P =.52). Conclusion: Liraglutide decreased the A- and B-type natriuretic peptides significantly in patients with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) and concomitant T2D, suggesting a beneficial mechanism of liraglutide in T2D patients with HFrEF.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Jorsal, Anders","Tougaard, Rasmus Stilling","Rasmussen, Jon J.","Schou, Morten","Videbaek, Lars","Gustafsson, Ida","Faber, Jens","Flyvbjerg, Allan","Wiggers, Henrik","Tarnow, Lise","Kistorp, Caroline"],"cuid":["S03-097.88-014420"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Nielsen, Roni"],"keyword":["Adrenomedullin","Biomarkers","Cardiovascular events","Copeptin","Double blind","GLP 1 analogue","Heart failure","Mortality","POTASSIUM EXCRETION","Prognostic value","Type 2 diabetes","URINARY SODIUM","neurohormones"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the impact of the glucagon like peptide 000000000001 receptor agonist liraglutide on natriuretic peptides in heart failure patients with reduced ejection fraction with and without type 000000000002 diabetes","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/290607207/The_impact_of_the_glucagon_like_peptide_1_receptor_agonist_liraglutide_on_natriuretic_peptides_in_heart_failure_patients_with_reduced_ejection_fraction_with_and_without_type_2_diabetes_accepted_version_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The current study was supported by an unrestricted grant from Novo Nordisk Inc., Denmark."],"id":"S03-097.88-014420","identifier":[{"id":"85089312606","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32627271","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000558674700001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-1946155d-7e3e-4438-8fd5-6f3dd90666de","ku-37877787-a65b-493f-988d-a10bbb4cdc6f","sdu-ea0d320c-654a-4359-a891-580842d6eaaa"],"keyword":["ADRENOMEDULLIN","BIOMARKERS","CARDIOVASCULAR EVENTS","COPEPTIN","DOUBLE-BLIND","GLP-1 analogue","MORTALITY","NEUROHORM