Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089312606"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-1946155d-7e3e-4438-8fd5-6f3dd90666de","ku-37877787-a65b-493f-988d-a10bbb4cdc6f","sdu-ea0d320c-654a-4359-a891-580842d6eaaa"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The impact of the glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist liraglutide on natriuretic peptides in heart failure patients with reduced ejection fraction with and without type 2 diabetes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-097.88-014420","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Background. Liraglutide, a glucagon-like peptide-1 agonist, is used for treatment of type 2 diabetes and has beneficial cardiovascular properties. However, treatment increases heart rate (HR) and possibly the risk of cardiovascular events in chronic heart failure (CHF) patients. We investigated potential associations between HR changes and clinical, laboratory and echocardiographic parameters and clinical events in liraglutide treated CHF patients. Methods. This was a sub-study of the LIVE study. CHF patients (N = 241) with a left ventricular ejection fraction ≤45% were randomised to 1.8 mg liraglutide daily or placebo for 24 weeks. Electrocardiograms (N = 117) and readouts from cardiac implanted electronic devices (N = 20) were analysed for HR and arrhythmias. Results. In patients with sinus rhythm (SR), liraglutide increased HR by 8 ± 9 bpm (pulse measurements), 9 ± 9 bpm (ECG measurements) and 9 ± 6 bpm (device readouts) versus placebo (all p'.005). Increases in HR correlated with liraglutide dose (p=.01). HR remained unchanged in patients without SR. Serious cardiac adverse events were not associated with HR changes. Conclusions. During 6 months of treatment, HR increased substantially in CHF patients with SR treated with liraglutide but was not associated with adverse events. The long-term clinical significance of increased HR in liraglutide treated CHF patients needs to be determined.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Tougaard, Rasmus Stilling","Jorsal, Anders","Tarnow, Lise","Hansson, Nils Henrik","Kistorp, Caroline","Schou, Morten","Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Flyvbjerg, Allan","Videbaek, Lars","Mølgaard, Henning","Nielsen, Jens Cosedis","Gustafsson, Ida","Wiggers, Henrik"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-030065"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Tougaard, Rasmus Stilling"],"keyword":["Arrhythmias","Cardiovascular events","Heart failure","Incretin hormones","Type 2 diabetes"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-STENO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"heart rate increases in liraglutide treated chronic heart failure patients association with clinical parameters and adverse events liraglutide increases heart rate only in chronic heart failure patients with sinus rhythm and independently of beta blocker dose","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85083499216","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-030065","identifier":[{"id":"32292074","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85083499216","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f02afeaa-62ac-4557-8faf-f485fffac813","ku-e47532ea-6618-4b20-9eff-f1b47b9b186b","sdu-d7d1a893-b4ba-45a1-a2d2-7154abce17b5"],"keyword":["Type 2 diabetes","arrhythmias","cardiovascular events","heart failure","incretin hormones"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-DE17706315DF0E01174BA8723750763B"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denma